namespace Ponca
{
/*!
  \page fitting_curvature_page Curvature estimation

  \tableofcontents

  \section fitting_curvature_intro Overview

  At a point of a smooth surface, the curvature of the surface in a tangent direction is measured by the normal
  curvature. Its extremal values are the *principal curvatures* \f$k_{\min} \leq k_{\max}\f$, reached along the
  orthogonal *principal directions*. The *mean curvature* is \f$H = (k_{\min} + k_{\max}) / 2\f$ and the
  *Gaussian curvature* is \f$K = k_{\min} k_{\max}\f$. On point clouds, these quantities can only be estimated over a
  neighborhood: the estimates depend on the scale \f$t\f$ of the analysis.

  %Ponca offers two families of estimators:
  - scalar estimators, related to the mean curvature (or to its magnitude),
  - curvature tensor estimators, which provide the principal curvatures and directions through the capability
    Ponca::ProvidesPrincipalCurvatures: `kmin()`, `kmax()`, `kminDirection()`, `kmaxDirection()`, `kMean()` and
    `GaussianCurvature()`.

  Most of them are reviewed and compared by Arnal-Anger et al. \cite ArnalAnger:2026:Survey.

  \section fitting_curvature Available estimators

  | Estimator                                                          | Input              | Estimated quantity  | Definition |
  |--------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------|---------------------|------------|
  | Distance to the PCA plane \cite Digne:2011:scalespacemeshing       | positions          | mean curvature (scaled by \f$t^2\f$, unsigned) | Ponca::Plane::potential() of: \snippet curvature_plane.cpp Curvature Estimator PCA plane |
  | Surface variation \cite Pauly:2002:PSSimplification                | positions          | curvature magnitude | Ponca::CovarianceBase::surfaceVariation() of the same basket |
  | Growing Least Squares \cite Mellado:2012:GLS                       | oriented points    | mean curvature      | Ponca::GLSParam::kappa() of `Basket<P, F, OrientedSphereFit, GLSParam>` |
  | Normal derivatives of the PCA plane (PSS \cite Alexa:2001:Pss)     | positions          | curvature tensor    | \snippet curvature_plane.cpp Curvature Tensor PCA normals |
  | Monge patch, quadratic height field (similar to jets \cite Cazals:2005:Jets) | positions | curvature tensor   | \snippet curvature_plane.cpp Curvature Estimator Monge Quadric |
  | Monge patch, restricted quadratic height field                     | positions          | curvature tensor    | \snippet curvature_plane.cpp Curvature Estimator Monge Quadric Restricted |
  | Algebraic Point Set Surfaces (APSS) \cite Guennebaud:2007:APSS     | oriented points    | curvature tensor    | \snippet fit_radius_curvature_center.cpp Curvature Estimator APSS |
  | Algebraic Shape Operator (ASO) \cite Lejemble:2021:stable          | oriented points    | curvature tensor    | \snippet fit_radius_curvature_center.cpp Curvature Estimator ASO |
  | Corrected Normal Current (CNC) \cite pointCloudCNC-sgp23           | oriented points    | curvature tensor    | \snippet cnc.cpp CNCFitType |

  In these snippets, `FitSmoothOriented` stands for `Basket<Point, WeightSmoothFunc, OrientedSphereFit>`. The
  tensor estimators are only available in 3D.

  - The curvature tensor of the APSS is obtained from the spatial derivatives of the normal of the fitted sphere
    (Ponca::OrientedSphereDer), and converted into principal curvatures by
    Ponca::NormalDerivativeWeingartenEstimator and Ponca::WeingartenCurvatureEstimatorDer. The ASO additionally
    accounts for the second order derivatives of the sphere (Ponca::MlsSphereFitDer), which makes it more stable
    \cite Lejemble:2021:stable.
  - Monge patches fit a height field over the PCA plane, and compute the curvatures from its fundamental forms
    (Ponca::FundamentalFormWeingartenEstimator).
  - CNC estimators build triangles from the neighbors and integrate the corrected normal current measures over them.
    The template parameter selects how the triangles are generated (Ponca::TriangleGenerationMethod):
    `UniformGeneration` and `IndependentGeneration` draw random triplets of neighbors, `HexagramGeneration` and
    `AvgHexagramGeneration` build two triangles from six sectors around the evaluation point.

  \warning In the current version, the curvatures obtained from the normal derivatives of the PCA plane
  (Ponca::CovariancePlaneDer) are multiplied by the sum of the weights of the neighborhood (`getWeightSum()`); their
  directions are not affected. This estimator is only tested on planar data.

  \section fitting_curvature_usage Usage

  Curvature tensor estimators are used as any other fit. With the ASO and a kd-tree:
  \code
  using Point      = Ponca::PointPositionNormal<double, 3>;
  using VectorType = Point::VectorType;
  using Filter     = Ponca::DistWeightFilter<Point, Ponca::SmoothWeightKernel<double>>;
  using ASO        = Ponca::BasketDiff<Ponca::Basket<Point, Filter, Ponca::OrientedSphereFit>, Ponca::FitSpaceDer,
                                       Ponca::OrientedSphereDer, Ponca::MlsSphereFitDer,
                                       Ponca::NormalDerivativeWeingartenEstimator,
                                       Ponca::WeingartenCurvatureEstimatorDer>;

  Ponca::KdTreeDense<Point> tree(points);
  for (const Point& point : tree.points())
  {
      ASO fit;
      fit.setNeighborFilter({point.pos(), t});
      if (fit.computeWithIds(tree.rangeNeighbors(point.pos(), t), tree.points()) == Ponca::STABLE)
      {
          double     k1 = fit.kmin(), k2 = fit.kmax();                    // principal curvatures, k1 <= k2
          VectorType d1 = fit.kminDirection(), d2 = fit.kmaxDirection(); // principal directions
          double     H  = fit.kMean(), K = fit.GaussianCurvature();
      }
  }
  \endcode

  Ponca::CNC is not a basket: its neighbor filter is constructed from a point, whose position is the evaluation
  position and whose normal is used to orient the triangles. Its `computeWithIds` function takes a container of
  indices (kd-tree queries must be copied first):
  \code
  using CNC = Ponca::CNC<Point, Ponca::HexagramGeneration>;

  std::vector<int> neighbors;
  for (int j : tree.rangeNeighbors(point.pos(), t))
      neighbors.push_back(j);

  CNC cnc;
  cnc.setNeighborFilter({point, t}); // position and normal of the evaluation point, radius
  if (cnc.computeWithIds(neighbors, tree.points()) == Ponca::STABLE)
      std::cout << cnc.kmin() << " " << cnc.kmax() << " " << cnc.kMean() << std::endl;
  \endcode
  The CNC neighbor filter uses a constant weight (Ponca::ConstantWeightKernel) in the ball of radius \f$t\f$. As the
  triangles of `UniformGeneration` and `IndependentGeneration` are drawn at random, two evaluations at the same point
  give slightly different results.

  \section fitting_curvature_sign Sign conventions

  The sign of the curvatures depends on the orientation of the normals:
  - for the estimators based on oriented points (GLS, APSS, ASO, CNC), the curvatures are positive when the surface
    bends away from the normals. A sphere of radius \f$r\f$ sampled with outward normals has \f$\kappa = k_{\min} =
    k_{\max} = 1/r\f$; with inward normals, all these values are negative;
  - the estimators based on positions only rely on the normal of the PCA plane, whose orientation is arbitrary.
    Monge patches use the opposite convention, relative to their normal `fit.primitiveGradient()`: a sphere gives
    \f$-1/r\f$ when this normal points outward. To compare such estimates with a reference orientation \f$\mathbf{n}\f$,
    flip the curvatures (and swap \f$k_{\min}\f$ and \f$k_{\max}\f$) when `fit.primitiveGradient().dot(n)` has the
    wrong sign;
  - unsigned quantities (surface variation, distance to the PCA plane) only measure the magnitude of the curvature.

  The definitions behind these conventions, and the formulas of each estimator, are given in
  \ref maths_curvature_page.

  The principal directions are tangent vectors, defined up to their sign. Normalize them before use: in the current
  version, the directions computed through Ponca::NormalDerivativeWeingartenEstimator (APSS, ASO, PCA normal
  derivatives) are not unit vectors (their norm is \f$\sqrt{3}\f$), whereas those of the Monge patches and of the CNC
  estimators are.

  \section fitting_curvature_scale Influence of the scale

  An estimate at scale \f$t\f$ summarizes the geometry of the whole neighborhood. Small scales capture fine details but
  are sensitive to noise and to the sampling, large scales are more robust but smooth the curvature out and bias it
  on curved surfaces. Figure 1 shows the GLS curvature computed at two scales on the same object.

  \image html buste.png "Figure 1. Mean curvature (GLSParam::kappa) computed at a fine (left) and a coarse (right) scale, and rendered with a simple color map (orange for concavities, blue for convexities)."

  The scale-space behavior of the GLS parameters is studied with their scale derivatives, see
  \ref fitting_derivatives.
 */
} // namespace Ponca
